{
  "dom_id": "step-q159827",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{17}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{17},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{17}{x}_{i}=8,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{17}({x}_{i})!=4.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=17$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=8,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=4,$ and $n=17.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-17\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
